 total and the variable cost  now let's think about what's happening  so first let's look at the average the  marginal actually let's look at the  marginal cost first because this is  interesting and this kind of goes in  with this narrative of at first those  first oranges that we bought were  expensive we weren't a major producer  but incrementally that incrementally as  we produce that those next oranges so as  we go from 1000 orange as we go from the  the orange is needed for 1 000 gallons  to the oranges needed for 2 000 gallons  all of a sudden our marginal cost went  down we are now a bigger buyer we can  now those incremental oranges are now  cheaper to us and as we get even more as  we go to 3 000 gallons worth of oranges  those incremental oranges get even  cheaper for us and then we have to go  and buy oranges from the outside so  those incremental oranges get more and  more and more and more expensive and  that's most clear in this marginal cost  curve and it's somewhat clear in the  average the average variable cost and in  the average variable cost it gets  muffled to a certain degree because  remember the average variable cost at  any one of these points and we  calculated it already several times the  average variable cost at any of these  points is equal to  is equal to your variable cost divided  by the gallons of juice so another way  to think about it referring to the last  video if you're taking any one of these  points your average variable cost is the  slope between that point and the origin  while the marginal cost is the slope  between that point and the previous  point so the marginal cost is really  showing how much are those next  incremental oranges costing you not just  how much are all of the oranges on  average costing you so that's why the  marginal cost curve captures captures  how much those you know that very next  set of a thousand gallons worth of  oranges how much that is costing you and  obviously your average total cost at  first you have your fixed costs aren't  being spread out too much but then as  you have higher as you get more and more  gallons of orange juice produced your  fixed costs get spread out more and more  and more and so you actually have this  gap this gap between the green curve and  the orange curve which is really your  average fixed cost that becomes lower  and lower and lower

